<p>Mexico is trying to steady public confidence after the killing of Nemesio “El Mencho” Oseguera—leader of the Jalisco New Generation Cartel (CJNG)—triggered a wave of retaliatory violence in several regions, including road blockades and attacks on security forces. President Claudia Sheinbaum said visitors face “no risk” and that there are “all guarantees” for matches Mexico will host at the 2026 FIFA World Cup. A FIFA spokesperson said the organization is closely monitoring the situation and has no plan to move the 13 matches scheduled in Mexico. Governments and travel coverage have also focused on near-term disruptions. Canada resumed flights, and Foreign Affairs Minister Anita Anand said the situation remained “very volatile” but appeared to be normalizing, urging Canadians to follow local authorities’ advice. Travel reporting said the unrest rattled tourists and prompted some to reconsider plans, as advisories in places like Guadalajara included instructions to shelter in place during the flare-up. The episode has also put cross-border security cooperation—and political pressure—back in the spotlight. U.S. officials told Reuters that American intelligence agencies, along with a newer U.S. military-led task force focused on cartel intelligence, helped support Mexico’s operation. The White House said it would respond harshly if cartels target U.S. citizens. Separate analysis described Mexico as navigating pressure from President Trump to confront cartels while also managing the risk of further violence and a potential power struggle among criminal groups after “El Mencho’s” death.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/E7okSj3dfpzFlgmOmXyrpDFvWQZOrXs2ypy5K4cHlcldHbeVYXI_k91GG3i9FJU9bwJfGsh662pRE4kkKrp9FryPSPHorVyib8mwC1FoRGEEoGsTcIiDBjs8S_Qb_wRtgQI6vY8tpDYMFJUTzIxfzRDnZeJq72WCr02RwAU1bhAoHq723s8yiZ2TEIzvINt9BZ_wTu1yrcHXolpmwTymbX7XzBi-YiHsVOiIgNHONH-4bKs_dp-Gyfyoj1e4QDSEOjum_v7VlJwE65h5Gw' alt='Mexican President Claudia Sheinbaum holds a morning press conference in Mexico City on Feb. 23, 2026, a day after the reported death of CJNG leader Nemesio &quot;El Mencho&quot; Oseguera.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>How he was found: Mexican security officials said they tracked “El Mencho” to a cabin/villa area by following a romantic partner/lover, which led to the operation that ended in his death.</li><li>Tourist-area setting: Reporting said his hideout was in Tapalpa, Jalisco—described as an ecotourism hotspot—and that the cabin location had been flagged by the U.S. Treasury.</li><li>Guadalajara fears: Residents in and around Guadalajara described anxiety about more violence after the rampage, even as daily routines began to return in some areas.</li><li>Host-city scrutiny: Coverage pointed to fresh doubts—and renewed debate—over Guadalajara’s ability to host major international events after the retaliatory attacks tied to CJNG.</li><li>Raid details: Reporting described a dawn attempt to detain “El Mencho” that led to a firefight; he was fatally wounded and died while being airlifted to hospital.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Claudia Sheinbaum (Mexico): Sheinbaum said there is “no risk” for World Cup visitors and that Mexico has “all guarantees,” while also saying the situation was normalizing after coordinated attacks. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/24/sheinbaum-world-cup-visitors-violence-el-mencho'>The Guardian</a>, <a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/sports/soccer/article-mexico-president-guarantees-2026-fifa-world-cup/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Foreign Affairs Minister Anita Anand (Canada): Anand described the security situation as “very volatile” but said it appeared to be normalizing as flights resumed, and she urged Canadians to follow local authorities’ advice. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/video-situation-in-mexico-is-very-volatile-but-appears-to-be-normalizing/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>FIFA: A FIFA spokesperson said the organization was closely monitoring the situation as Mexico prepares to host matches. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/mexico-president-says-there-are-all-guarantees-for-2026-world-cup'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>White House (United States): The White House confirmed the operation in Tapalpa, Jalisco, used U.S. intelligence support and vowed a harsh response if cartels target U.S. citizens. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/24/white-house-vows-harsh-response-if-mexican-cartels-target-us-citizens-after-el-mencho-deat'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>Travel guidance writers: Travel coverage said the flare-up rattled tourists and led some to reconsider trips, with some areas under instructions to shelter in place during the violence. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/travel/mexico-tourism-safety-spring-break.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/video-situation-in-mexico-is-very-volatile-but-appears-to-be-normalizing/'>Situation in Mexico is very volatile, but appears to be normalizing, Anand says</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/24/sheinbaum-world-cup-visitors-violence-el-mencho'>Mexico pledges safety for World Cup after violence erupts from cartel boss’s killing</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgml2djwwvno'>Threat of further violence looms after Mexican cartel rampage</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/travel/mexico-tourism-safety-spring-break.html'>Is It Safe to Travel to Mexico Right Now, Given the Cartel Violence?</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/mexico-president-says-there-are-all-guarantees-for-2026-world-cup'>Mexico president says ‘no risk’ for 2026 World Cup fans</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/cartel-violence-fuels-worries-about-fifa-world-cup-games-in-mexico'>Cartel viol